            <span id="alg1.l26.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><math id="alg1.l26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{j*}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{j*}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l26.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> update with </span><math id="alg1.l26.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">′′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l26.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> from </span><span id="alg1.l26.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">train-rescaler</span><span id="alg1.l26.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><math id="alg1.l26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l26.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">) </span><span id="alg1.l26.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math>  Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.2.2 Training the Rescaling Transform ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l27" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l27.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">27:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l27.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><math id="alg1.l27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{stats}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">stats</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{stats}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l27.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> update with </span><span id="alg1.l27.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">find-min-rescaled-q</span><span id="alg1.l27.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><math id="alg1.l27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l27.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">) </span><span id="alg1.l27.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math>  Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
  </span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l28" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l28.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">28:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l28.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg1.l28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l28.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Constructed from globally best model </span><math id="alg1.l28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l28.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (and associated values, e.g., </span><math id="alg1.l28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l28.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">) and </span><math id="alg1.l28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{stats}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">stats</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{stats}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l28.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l29" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l29.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">29:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l29.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg1.l29.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">return</span><span id="alg1.l29.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">tr</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l29.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l30" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l30.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">30:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">tr</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l30.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The estimator that rejects all points is index-conditional calibrated. Given two estimators that are index-conditional calibrated, we prefer that which rejects fewer points, ceteris paribus. In other words, we seek estimators that meet our reliability condition and are informative (i.e., maximize the number of points that are properly admitted), but when the estimator is uncertain, we prefer rejection over unexpectedly falling under the desired <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> probability threshold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The key compromise is that we will not be able to reliably calculate a probability for all points; however, for LLM tasks, there is typically not an actionable notion of partial acceptability for final decision-making, so it is a reasonable compromise. Either the complex LLM output is verified as correct, or some separate, remedial action must be taken, such as dividing the task into simpler tasks, reformatting and re-cross-encoding, and/or retrieving information exogenous to the model, where again for each of these sub-tasks, we seek index-conditional calibrated estimators at the level of the available labels, where the stopping condition is eventually deferment to human adjudication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the aforementioned compromise, and although evaluation is unambiguous, it may still seem mysterious that the second condition of Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> can be meaningfully estimated. To do so, we will need to perform a series of transforms over the already strong uncertainty signals from the <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation function and re-visit the behavior of partitioning empirical CDFs, to which we turn next.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A disadvantage of using <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> directly as an estimator is that it only has an indirect, relative notion of the effective sample size of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, the confidence in a prediction should be commensurate with the number of comparable points in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, which the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation captures via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, an out-of-distribution point will tend to have <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0</annotation></semantics></math> and low values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting a small effective sample size in the observed data. However, to further improve the robustness of the estimate, we can explicitly incorporate an additional, direct notion of the effective sample size via distributional statistics over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we calculate class-conditional empirical CDFs over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> of the output of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given point, this will create a vector, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, of the quantiles:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}=\left[{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1}),\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}=\left[{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1}),\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Next, we rescale <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> to take into account these distributional statistics. The resulting value will be the basis for our stable mapping between new, unseen test points and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{\hat{y}}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{\hat{y}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We seek a normalized distribution both to present to users and to enable the subsequent transform described in § <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS3" title="4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.3</span></a>. Toward this end, we rescale with a linear layer, without a bias, the training of which we detail in § <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS2" title="4.2.2 Training the Rescaling Transform ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.2</span></a>: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}},{\bm{v}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}},{\bm{v}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is normalized using <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> as the base, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{i}=\frac{(2+\softqbin)^{~v^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbin)^{~v^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{i}=\frac{(2+\softqbin)^{~v^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbin)^{~v^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike the output of an <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily (but typically will be) equivalent to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. When they are not equivalent, our convention is to set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin=0</annotation></semantics></math> for the point, which will in effect treat the point as out-of-distribution in downstream analyses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> is premised on the assumption that the empirical CDFs in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reflect the true, underlying conditional distributions, which are unspecified.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                  
                  
                  
                That is also true for the eCDFs in Eq <a href="#S4.E6" title='In 4.1.3 "Distance" ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to SDM Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, but we make the reasonable assumption that the higher-level transforms starting with Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> effectively account for the uncertainty in the distance eCDFs.</span></span></span> That would seem to be a relatively strong assumption as the final estimate, particularly for small sample sizes, even if empirically effective over existing datasets, and is the entry point for incorporating an explicit notion of the effective sample size in our estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We make the following conservative assumption, parameterizing the prior belief that data points with a looser connection to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> reflect smaller effective sample sizes, while also explicitly accounting for the count of observed points in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume the effective sample size is increasing in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math>, class-wise over <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, using <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math>, we calculate the vector of effective sample sizes across classes, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, relative to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{n}}=\left[|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{1}}\cdot{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\softqbin),\ldots,|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{C}}\cdot{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\softqbin)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{n}}=\left[|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{1}}\cdot{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\softqbin),\ldots,|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{C}}\cdot{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\softqbin)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\calibrationSplit|^{y_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the count of calibration set points with true label <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With these sample size estimates, we can then construct a band around the empirical CDFs using the sharp constant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Massart, 1990</a>)</cite> of the distribution-free DKW inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dvoretzky et al., 1956</a>)</cite>, calculating the error for each class <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the corresponding index in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{n}_{c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{n}_{c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon_{c}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2\cdot\hat{n}_{c}}\log_{e}\left(\frac{2}{1-\alpha^{\prime}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon_{c}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2\cdot\hat{n}_{c}}\log_{e}\left(\frac{2}{1-\alpha^{\prime}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{n}_{c}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{n}_{c}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, our convention is to set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then construct the lower and upper counterparts to the quantile vector of Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}=[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}=[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1})-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=1}\cdot\epsilon_{1}+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq 1}\cdot\epsilon_{1},0\right),1\right),\ldots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1})-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=1}\cdot\epsilon_{1}+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq 1}\cdot\epsilon_{1},0\right),1\right),\ldots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=C}\cdot\epsilon_{C}+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq C}\cdot\epsilon_{C},0\right),1\right)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=C}\cdot\epsilon_{C}+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq C}\cdot\epsilon_{C},0\right),1\right)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}=[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>upper</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}=[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1})+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=1}\cdot\epsilon_{1}-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq 1}\cdot\epsilon_{1},0\right),1\right),\ldots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{1})+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=1}\cdot\epsilon_{1}-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq 1}\cdot\epsilon_{1},0\right),1\right),\ldots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=C}\cdot\epsilon_{C}-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq C}\cdot\epsilon_{C},0\right),1\right)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\left(\max\left({\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{C})+\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=C}\cdot\epsilon_{C}-\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}\neq C}\cdot\epsilon_{C},0\right),1\right)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">from which <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinLower</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinUpper</annotation></semantics></math> follow:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\softqbinLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\softqbinLower</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{{\rm{lower}}_{\hat{y}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><msub><mi>lower</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></msub></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{{\rm{lower}}_{\hat{y}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\softqbinUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\softqbinUpper</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{{\rm{upper}}_{\hat{y}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><msub><mi>upper</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></msub></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=log_{e}\left((2+q)^{{\bm{v}}_{{\rm{upper}}_{\hat{y}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Analogous to Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, we then construct our estimators after rescaling <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>lower</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>lower</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}_{\rm{lower}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>upper</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}T}{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>upper</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}_{\rm{upper}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pLower</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(2+\softqbinLower)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{lower}}_{\hat{y}}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbinLower)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{lower}}_{c}}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>lower</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>lower</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(2+\softqbinLower)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{lower}}_{\hat{y}}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbinLower)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{lower}}_{c}}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pCentroid</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=o_{\hat{y}}~~~\rhd\text{from Eq.~\ref{equation:rescaled-prediction-conditional-estimate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊳</mo><mrow><mtext>from Eq. </mtext><mtext><a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=o_{\hat{y}}~~~\rhd\text{from Eq.~\ref{equation:rescaled-prediction-conditional-estimate}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pUpper</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(2+\softqbinUpper)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{upper}}_{\hat{y}}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbinUpper)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{upper}}_{c}}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>upper</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>upper</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(2+\softqbinUpper)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{upper}}_{\hat{y}}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbinUpper)^{~v^{\prime}_{{\rm{upper}}_{c}}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As with Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, the convention is to set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinLower=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinLower=0</annotation></semantics></math> and/or <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinUpper=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinUpper=0</annotation></semantics></math> for the rare cases for which the transforms in Eq. <a href="#S4.E18" title="In Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality. ‣ 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> and/or Eq. <a href="#S4.E20" title="In Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality. ‣ 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>, respectively, result in the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> value of the normalized output vector not being equivalent to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. (In such cases, e.g., Eq. <a href="#S4.E18" title="In Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality. ‣ 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> is not re-calculated with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinLower=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinLower=0</annotation></semantics></math>, but rather such values are treated separately in downstream analyses as out-of-distribution points.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base Estimators.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower\in\mathbb{R}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower\in\mathbb{R}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> will be used as the basis of our primary test-time estimator of prediction-conditional uncertainty (see § <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS5" title="4.2.5 Index-Conditional Calibration ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.5</span></a> for the complete, index-conditional estimator). <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid\in\mathbb{R}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid\in\mathbb{R}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (via Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is a consequence of intermediate results needed in service of constructing <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., for training the re-scaler and setting a threshold on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math>, described below), whereas <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pUpper\in\mathbb{R}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pUpper\in\mathbb{R}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is primarily only of research interest, included here to analyze the behavior of the approach.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                  
                  
                  
                In practice, rather than using <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pUpper</annotation></semantics></math>, if a less stringent admission criteria is desired, the operative action is to reduce <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and re-estimate <math id="footnote6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Training the Rescaling Transform</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the re-scaling linear layer over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>) by minimizing the following loss (Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.2.2 Training the Rescaling Transform ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which is the counterpart to Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, while all other parameters remain fixed:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)=-\frac{1}{|\calibrationSplit|}\sum_{n}^{|\calibrationSplit|}\log_{(2+\softqbin)}\left(\frac{(2+\softqbin)^{v^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbin)^{v^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.980em">ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)=-\frac{1}{|\calibrationSplit|}\sum_{n}^{|\calibrationSplit|}\log_{(2+\softqbin)}\left(\frac{(2+\softqbin)^{v^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+\softqbin)^{v^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our convention is to train with a batch size of 1 and conclude the learning process if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)</annotation></semantics></math> increases for a pre-specified (as a hyper-parameter) number of consecutive epochs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Training the Weights of the Rescaling Transform</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">cached </span><math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><span id="alg2.l1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">rescaler max epochs</span><span id="alg2.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><span id="alg2.l1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">rescaler stopping condition</span><span id="alg2.l1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">procedure</span><span id="alg2.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">train-rescaler</span><span id="alg2.l2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(cached </span><math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, rescaler max epochs, rescaler stopping condition)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">′′</mo></msup></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Final weights
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">′′</mo></msup></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> random initialization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rm{metric}\leftarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">metric</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rm{metric}\leftarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rm{counter}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">counter</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rm{counter}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg2.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="alg2.l7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in 1,\ldots,\text{rescaler max epochs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">rescaler max epochs</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in 1,\ldots,\text{rescaler max epochs}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg2.l7.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Minimize </span><math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm{loss}}\leftarrow{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">loss</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑾</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">′′</mo></msup></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{loss}}\leftarrow{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}^{{}^{\prime\prime}};\calibrationSplit)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg2.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Eq. <a href="#S4.E21" title="In 4.2.2 Training the Rescaling Transform ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span id="alg2.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">if</span><span id="alg2.l9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{loss}}&lt;\rm{metric}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">loss</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">metric</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{loss}}&lt;\rm{metric}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">then</span><span id="alg2.l9.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
