<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Region-specific eCDFs</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The estimator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> incorporates an explicit notion of the effective sample size. Smaller effective sample sizes will be associated with lower probability estimates (and vice-versa). It also has a strong relative notion of the highest probability regions of the output distribution by virtue of the original <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> signals, and the aggregated distributional statistics over these signals. However, it lacks a human interpretable, principled cutoff, or threshold, by which we can have some assurance that the new points we see are reasonably comparable to the data we observed in deriving our estimator. This is a more subtle and foundational problem than it may initially seem; we must account for distribution shifts if we seek to realistically achieve our desired notion of index-conditional calibration (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). It will require an additional set of transforms to resolve, even with the already strong signals of prediction-conditional uncertainty from our estimator, to which we turn next.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that the output of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})=\triangle^{C-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})=\triangle^{C-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C-1</annotation></semantics></math>)-dimension simplex, where the dimension reduction is a consequence of the output summing to 1. The same is true of the normalized value <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we instead consider the over-parameterized version in which each event probability of the categorical distribution (e.g., Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is explicitly specified as an element of a vector of length <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, the following indicator result directly follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> class-conditional CDFs over categorical distributions where the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1])</annotation></semantics></math> quantile threshold <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\psi_{c}\in[0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\psi_{c}\in[0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> of each class <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>inverseCDF</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">∀</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.608em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>), a set of i.i.d. points sampled from the same distribution as the CDFs, each of whose event probability vector <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}=[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}=[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math> has one (1) element at least the corresponding class threshold (i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the comparison taken element-wise), will have class-conditional accuracies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, in expectation.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Partition the class-conditional CDFs of the categorical distributions, for which
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>inverseCDF</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">∀</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.608em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>, at <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting high-probability partitions—those <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\psi_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\psi_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>—are <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> Bernoulli distributions each with success probability <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c}\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c}\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Take as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n_{1},\ldots n_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n_{1},\ldots n_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math> the class-wise count of i.i.d. points whose event probability vector, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by the definition of the expected value of a Binomial distributed random variable, it follows from these trials that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\frac{n_{1}\cdot p_{c}}{n_{1}},\ldots,\frac{n_{C}\cdot p_{C}}{n_{C}}]=[\geq\alpha^{\prime},\ldots,\geq\alpha^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\frac{n_{1}\cdot p_{c}}{n_{1}},\ldots,\frac{n_{C}\cdot p_{C}}{n_{C}}]=[\geq\alpha^{\prime},\ldots,\geq\alpha^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the desired class-conditional accuracy for this restricted set of points. Now, instead assume that one or more of the Bernoulli distributions has a success probability <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c}&lt;\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c}&lt;\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that the class-conditional CDFs were constructed from a distribution whose event probabilities are not those of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimension simplex since we require <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>inverseCDF</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">∀</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.608em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})&gt;\frac{1}{C}~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the CDFs constructed class-wise relative to the true labels, which is a contradiction of the definition of a categorical distribution since the sum of all event probabilities, each of which is a real value in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, must equal 1.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that when <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}&lt;\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}&lt;\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math> no such assurance across all classes necessarily results, since the resulting thresholding of the probability vectors may induce a complex dependence across the class-conditional CDFs.<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                
                
                
              We leave to future work whether a similar result holds for a subset of the class-conditional accuracies if only <span id="footnote7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">some</span>, rather than <span id="footnote7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span>, class-wise thresholds are at least <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our primary verification setting over LLMs, the typical setting is <math id="footnote7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math>, or some similarly small <math id="footnote7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2+}</annotation></semantics></math>, where to ensure deployment reliability, the LLM would not be deployed until the verification task yields class-conditional accuracies for all classes at or above <math id="footnote7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the available labeled sets, so we do not consider this case here.</span></span></span> In such cases, the thresholding of a new point may result in multiple classes above the threshold, and the subsequent stratification of this set of points to those for which <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|[e_{1},\ldots e_{C}]\geq[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math> will not necessarily have class-conditional accuracies <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, in expectation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> thus differs from set-valued estimators such as conformal estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vovk et al., 2005</a>)</cite>, which as previously mentioned (see § <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, introduction) are premised on a different calibration compromise. For example, with conformal estimators, there is a statistical assurance for coverage of the true class in a discrete prediction set (itself a distinct quantity from that considered here) across all points regardless of the distribution of the conformity score (e.g., instead of a categorical distribution, a conformity score can be an unnormalized scoring function), but no assurance conditional on the subset of high-probability points. We explore the implications of these tradeoffs in our empirical experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> can be viewed as a useful indicator function, but it is not particularly informative as an estimator alone. We will use it in service of dividing the output distribution into high probability regions via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math>, described next.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, higher values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to points with a closer connection to the observed data and thus lower epistemic uncertainty, as this single value takes into account the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> signals, and distributional statistics over those signals. The result in Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> provides a principled basis for setting a threshold on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> that we can then apply at test time, without access to the true label, to constrain our estimates to the high-probability region of the distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The value of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> is real-valued, but only <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq|\calibrationSplit|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq|\calibrationSplit|</annotation></semantics></math> values are observed, so a simple iterative search algorithm is sufficient to find the value of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> such that all thresholds, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, over the estimates of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), are at least <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}&gt;\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}&gt;\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, so this more stringent requirement satisfies the condition in Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, while also requiring <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math> to be restricted to the prediction-conditional estimates of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The full algorithm appears in Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, iteratively constructing class-wise eCDFs over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to progressively larger values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin</annotation></semantics></math>. (These eCDFs over the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are only needed for Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and are not needed at test time, unlike those of Eq. <a href="#S4.E6" title='In 4.1.3 "Distance" ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to SDM Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, and Eq. <a href="#S4.E12" title="In Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality. ‣ 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.) Note that we only consider values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, as points with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin=0</annotation></semantics></math> are considered out-of-distribution.<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
                  
                  
                  
                The reason for the floor operation becomes evident in the next section. <math id="footnote8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math> will serve as our hard-partitioned mapping between the observed data and new test points to enable estimates of uncertainty over iterations of the entire process described thus far.</span></span></span> The search algorithm may fail to find a suitable final value, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math>, at which point the operative conclusion is that reliable estimates of index-conditional calibration (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) are not possible without reducing <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, or acquiring additional data and/or a stronger model.<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> could be readily modified to find an adaptive value of <math id="footnote9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, iteratively reducing <math id="footnote9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if a suitable <math id="footnote9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> value is not found. However, in practice, determining <math id="footnote9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for LLM settings is a decision made exogenous to the model development process. We seek to develop our models (and data) to meet a given <math id="footnote9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> value, rather than the other way around, so we do not consider that variation here.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When a value of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> can be found, the convention is to restrict our estimates of index-conditional calibration to the new, unseen test points that satisfy <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinLower\geq\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinLower\geq\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> after considering the final additional sources of uncertainty from the data splitting and learning processes, which we consider next.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Search Algorithm to Find <math id="alg3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> to Detect High-Probability Regions</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">cached </span><math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\softqbin,{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\softqbin,{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">procedure</span><span id="alg3.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">find-min-rescaled-q</span><span id="alg3.l2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(cached </span><math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\softqbin,{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\softqbin,{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg3.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg3.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> A suitable <math id="alg3.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> may not exist.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\leftarrow[\emptyset,\ldots,\emptyset]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\leftarrow[\emptyset,\ldots,\emptyset]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Needed at test-time, if applicable
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{q}s\leftarrow{\rm{sorted}}~[\tilde{q}\in\calibrationSplit~{\rm{s.t.}}~\hardqbin&gt;0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">sorted</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.310em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.477em">.</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}s\leftarrow{\rm{sorted}}~[\tilde{q}\in\calibrationSplit~{\rm{s.t.}}~\hardqbin&gt;0]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg3.l5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Restricted to <math id="alg3.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to exclude OOD
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">6:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg3.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="alg3.l6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin^{\prime}\in\tilde{q}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin^{\prime}\in\tilde{q}s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg3.l6.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">7:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Construct </span><math id="alg3.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}},\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">eCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">eCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}},\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for all </span><math id="alg3.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbin\geq\softqbin^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbin\geq\softqbin^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> in </span><math id="alg3.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> eCDFs for <math id="alg3.l7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), stratified by <math id="alg3.l7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Calculate </span><math id="alg3.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">inverseCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.477em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.477em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.588em" mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Quantile functions are inverses of L. <a href="#alg3.l7" title="In Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">9:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span id="alg3.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">if</span><span id="alg3.l9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{all}}(~[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\geq\alpha^{\prime}~)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">all</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.310em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{all}}(~[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\geq\alpha^{\prime}~)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">then</span><span id="alg3.l9.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l9.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Element-wise comparison
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">10:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">      </span><math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin\leftarrow\softqbin^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin\leftarrow\softqbin^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg3.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Satisfies Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 4.5. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> at the prediction-conditional estimate (see text) of <math id="alg3.l10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">11:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">      </span><span id="alg3.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">break</span><span id="alg3.l11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
     </span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">12:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg3.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg3.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">return</span><span id="alg3.l12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg3.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l12.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg3.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l12.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">13:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <math id="alg3.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg3.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4 </span>Accounting for Uncertainty in the Data Splitting and Learning Processes</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a final step, we take into account uncertainty over the data splitting and learning processes. This will incur non-trivial additional computational costs, but these are one-time development costs for an estimator. At test time, our estimates will be constant offsets on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math>, the latter conditional on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{0+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{0+}</annotation></semantics></math>, which will serve as a stable mapping between <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and new, unseen test points. In summary, in this section, we seek:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\CauchyMinValidQBin\hskip 20.00003pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\CauchyMinValidQBin\hskip 20.00003pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rhd\text{A robust estimate of $\minValidQBin$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊳</mo><mrow><mtext>A robust estimate of </mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rhd\text{A robust estimate of $\minValidQBin$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\hskip 20.00003pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\hskip 20.00003pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rhd\text{A class-wise, robust correction for $\pLower$, conditional on $\hardqbin$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊳</mo><mrow><mtext>A class-wise, robust correction for </mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mtext>, conditional on </mtext><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rhd\text{A class-wise, robust correction for $\pLower$, conditional on $\hardqbin$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conceptually, the estimation process is straightforward. We repeat the training and estimation processes described above <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> times and derive our constant offsets via summary statistics over those estimates. The one complication that arises is that we will have to depart from the distribution-assumption-light approaches above, since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> will typically not be large due to the computational expense. (The full process across <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> iterations to construct a single estimator needs to remain reasonably computationally lightweight relative to an LLM training epoch, as it itself will be embedded into the training loop of an LLM, described below.) Instead, we will estimate each of these processes as a Cauchy distribution, given its relatively wide tails and relatively robust scale parameter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A Cauchy distribution is defined by a location parameter, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionLocation" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionLocation</annotation></semantics></math>, and a scale parameter, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionScale" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionScale</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\CauchyDistribution(\CauchyDistributionLocation,\CauchyDistributionScale)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cauchy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\CauchyDistribution(\CauchyDistributionLocation,\CauchyDistributionScale)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The inverse CDF (i.e., quantile function) of a Cauchy distribution for a particular quantile, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, can be calculated analytically as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(\CauchyDistributionLocation,\CauchyDistributionScale)}(\alpha)=\CauchyDistributionLocation+\CauchyDistributionScale\tan\left(\pi\left(\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>inverseCDF</mi><mrow><mi>Cauchy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(\CauchyDistributionLocation,\CauchyDistributionScale)}(\alpha)=\CauchyDistributionLocation+\CauchyDistributionScale\tan\left(\pi\left(\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We take as our estimate of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionScale" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionScale</annotation></semantics></math> the median absolute deviation around the median of our sample (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMAD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMAD</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robust detection of high-probability regions.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To calculate <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionScale" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionScale</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin\in\mathbb{R}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin\in\mathbb{R}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMAD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMAD</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> estimates of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math>. The location parameter is taken as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimate of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\minValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\minValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> over the model with the final chosen weights (see Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We can then analytically calculate our desired value via Eq. <a href="#S4.E25" title="In 4.2.4 Accounting for Uncertainty in the Data Splitting and Learning Processes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a> at <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\CauchyMinValidQBin={\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(\minValidQBin_{*},\CauchyDistributionScale)}(\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>inverseCDF</mi><mrow><mi>Cauchy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.980em">min</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\CauchyMinValidQBin={\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(\minValidQBin_{*},\CauchyDistributionScale)}(\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the right-tail of the distribution, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin\geq\minValidQBin_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin\geq\minValidQBin_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a more restrictive threshold on the high-probability region. In scenarios (not considered in the experiments here) where the computational budget necessitates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the convention would be to take <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin\coloneq\minValidQBin_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mmultiscripts><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin\coloneq\minValidQBin_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a tacit assumption that these additional sources of uncertainty have not been explicitly accounted for.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robust output adjustment.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To calculate <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionScale" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionScale</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionScale\,|\,\hat{y},\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.448em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionScale\,|\,\hat{y},\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math>), we take the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMAD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMAD</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">medians</span> (as written) of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, conditional on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">10</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Implementation note: Unlike the eCDFs, which are constructed by stratifying on the true label, <math id="footnote10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="footnote10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, this quantity is calculated by stratifying on the predicted label, <math id="footnote10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="footnote10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> Similar to above, we can then calculate:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}={\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(0,(\CauchyDistributionScale\,|\,\hat{y},\hardqbin))}(\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>inverseCDF</mi><mrow><mi>Cauchy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}={\rm{inverseCDF}}_{\CauchyDistribution(0,(\CauchyDistributionScale\,|\,\hat{y},\hardqbin))}(\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In this case, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyDistributionLocation" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyDistributionLocation</annotation></semantics></math> is 0, as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}</annotation></semantics></math> will be subtracted from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> as an offset, an assumption that each distribution is centered on the given point. To simplify the presentation (and since the upper offset is not needed in practice), we only consider this as a lower offset on our base estimators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the number of points in the sample will tend to decrease, but so will the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMAD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMAD</annotation></semantics></math>, so the estimates remain reasonable in practice. As we will see in our experiments, high values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math> (that are otherwise attested in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>) are not uncommonly associated with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMAD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMAD</annotation></semantics></math> values that are within 0 of numerical precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math>, although it is generally recommend to take these additional sources of uncertainty into consideration, when <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the convention would be to take <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\coloneq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\coloneq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.5 </span>Index-Conditional Calibration</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the above models and estimators, we can now robustly calculate the index-conditional uncertainty of a new, unseen test point <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first take as the prediction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> to calculate <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math>; the cached class-wise empirical CDFs over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. <a href="#S4.E6" title='In 4.1.3 "Distance" ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to SDM Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, and Eq. <a href="#S4.E12" title="In Effective Sample Sizes via the DKW Inequality. ‣ 4.2.1 Rescaling SDM Activation Output to Account for Effective Sample Sizes ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>; <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> and the thresholds (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math>); and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the index-conditional uncertainty estimate of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\EstimatorPLower=\begin{cases}\max(0,\pLower-\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}})&amp;\text{if~}\left[\softqbinLower\geq\CauchyMinValidQBin\right]\wedge\left[\left(\pLower-\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\right)\geq\psi_{\hat{y}}\right]\\
\bot&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo>[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>]</mo><mo>∧</mo><mo>[</mo><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mo>⊥</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\EstimatorPLower=\begin{cases}\max(0,\pLower-\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}})&amp;\text{if~}\left[\softqbinLower\geq\CauchyMinValidQBin\right]\wedge\left[\left(\pLower-\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}\right)\geq\psi_{\hat{y}}\right]\\
\bot&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a rejected (non-admitted) point.<span id="footnote11" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">11</span>
                
                
                
              At test time, the mapping to the <math id="footnote11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math>-conditioned statistics (i.e., <math id="footnote11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is via <math id="footnote11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbinLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbinLower</annotation></semantics></math> for the test instance.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in the previous sections, in the rare cases when the transforms after the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation result in the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> index not matching <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softqbinLower=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softqbinLower=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which effectively treats the point as out-of-distribution. In such cases, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\EstimatorPLower=\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower=\bot</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> as a consequence of Line <a href="#alg3.l5" title="In Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Corralling the high-probability region via exclusion of the observed high-epistemic-uncertainty points. ‣ 4.2.3 Region-specific eCDFs ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our convention in subsequent sections will be to refer to summary statistics and comparisons of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E28" title="In 4.2.5 Index-Conditional Calibration ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>), excluding the points assigned <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math>, as estimates from the “estimator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower</annotation></semantics></math>”. We do the same for the “estimator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPCentroid</annotation></semantics></math>” and the “estimator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPUpper</annotation></semantics></math>”, but where the latter two quantities are calculated from the corresponding centroid and upper intermediate quantities, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complexity.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The added computational overhead over an underlying <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> with a <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> activation is dominated by calculating <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> (and by extension, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math>). The transforms after the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation function add negligible additional overhead. For perspective, this is on the order of the additional computation needed for commonly used dense retrieval augmentations of LLMs, so it is readily achievable at interactive speeds in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sharpness.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in § <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we seek estimators that are both informative (i.e., not unnecessarily rejecting correct predictions) and robust (i.e., we prefer rejection over falling under the expected <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy). The above transforms seek to achieve this by taking the uncertainty signals from an <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation and further separating the high and low probability regions of the distribution, as well as providing a hard cut via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin</annotation></semantics></math> to altogether exclude predictions over high epistemic uncertainty regions. We explore these behaviors empirically in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we incorporate our estimators directly into LLM next-token training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above approach is already a very powerful and easily implemented mechanism for building complex LLM pipelines. We can treat an underlying <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> as fixed, add an <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer, and then use the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator for conditional branching for test-time compute, retrieval, tool-calling, and related.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, earlier in the model development pipeline (e.g., as done by LLM model providers), we need a mechanism for fine-tuning a <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> after the initial unsupervised training stage.<span id="footnote12" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">12</span>
              
              
              
            In principle, the methods in this section can also be used for bootstrapping a randomly initialized LLM against an existing (possibly larger) model, which we leave to future work.</span></span></span> In this section, we show how to incorporate the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> mechanism directly into the LLM next-token training process. We will refer to this process and the resulting model as an <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Conceptually, an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation and estimator over an averaged history of frozen hidden states and the token-level hidden state will be trained for binary classification at the unit of analysis of the available labels (e.g., the document-level). This estimator then provides the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math> values for an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation for next-token loss of the LLM during training. Because an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation does not alter the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> prediction, greedy token-level generation can proceed without the computational cost of the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation at every token at test time, with the global <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator providing verification over the final generation. <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This process shares the same goal of existing fine-tuning approaches to increase overall accuracy, add information to a model, etc., as well as the new goal of increasing the proportion of verifiable high-probability generations from a model.</span> During training, we seek to penalize the model for verification mistakes, and reward the model for increasing the cardinality of the set of admitted points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce our data encoding scheme in § <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS1" title="4.3.1 Universal Verification Encoding ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.1</span></a> for verification. Next, orthogonal to the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> mechanism itself, we introduce a parsimonious regularization method (§ <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2 Negative+Positive Vocabulary Normalization and Regularization ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a>) to enable fine-tuning on a small amount of data while discouraging catastrophic forgetting. Finally, we introduce the process for training the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network (§ <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS3" title="4.3.3 SDM network ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Universal Verification Encoding</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the abstract, our data is similar to that in the previous sections: Input documents accompanied with discrete labels. However, while we previously treated each document, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as a single atomic unit, we will now also be concerned with the individual tokens of the document, for which we use the notation <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}],y_{n},[y_{n}^{\rm{task}}])\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>task</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}],y_{n},[y_{n}^{\rm{task}}])\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for our labeled training set, and similarly for our labeled calibration set, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. Each token, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is represented as an index into a vocabulary, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary of the LLM trained during the initial unsupervised training stage. The reason for the factor of 2 is described in the next section. Implicit in our representation is that each instance will have a marker at some <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating a “completion” (i.e., a sequence after an instruction prompt or prefix, more generally). Our document-level labels, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, are as in previous sections, but specifically restricted to binary classification, where the convention is to treat <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> as representing the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unverified</span> class and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">verified</span> class (i.e., an acceptable generation, conditional on the instruction or context).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For some documents, we have classification labels, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2+}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the underlying tasks encoded in the data. For example, for a sentiment classification task of negative and positive reviews, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> for verification when the classification decision is wrong, whereas <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> for verification when the classification decision is correct. Among those for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask=0</annotation></semantics></math> could indicate a negative review and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask=1</annotation></semantics></math> could indicate a positive review. These task-specific labels are predicted via the generated text of the LLM, and if available, we can use them during training (e.g., as part of our stopping criteria to choose the best weights, by parsing the generated text and comparing to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math>). Unlike typical classification settings, these labels may—and typically will—cover multiple disparate tasks; hence, the designation of <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">universal</span> verification. When the distinction is potentially ambiguous, we will add a superscript to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for the binary verification labels: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yVerification</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike typical preference fine-tuning encodings, we do not require prefixes (or prompts) of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be paired with different completions and opposing document-level labels. However, as in the above sections, we will assume that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are balanced across <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., an approximately equal number of documents with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer for verification will be trained with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, seeing all documents with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> labels. However, the LLM’s <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation for next-token training will only directly see documents with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the signal of the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unverified</span> class coming indirectly via matching into <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> to calculate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>. (There is an additional nuance with train-time generation vs. train-time force-decoding that will be clarified below.) As such, the additional <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> mechanisms enable a unification of preference fine-tuning, instruction fine-tuning, and supervised fine-tuning encodings since in all of the above, we always have at least the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> documents, and it is typically straightforward to collect, or otherwise synthetically generate, unpaired examples to serve as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., generations we seek to avoid showing users).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Negative+Positive Vocabulary Normalization and Regularization</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before we can make progress on incorporating the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> mechanisms, we need to address the matter of fine-tuning pre-trained LLMs without inducing catastrophic forgetting. This is critical, since each round of LLM training and fine-tuning is computationally expensive. We seek to make incremental changes to the model without having to run subsequent learning processes over all previously seen data. To address this, we first briefly recall the training of auto-regressive neural language models prior to the era of large-scale pre-training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">[L]LM Training Redux.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior to the era of large-scale pre-training of LMs that emerged at the end of the 2010’s, auto-regressive language models for transduction tasks (e.g., grammatical error correction) were successfully trained from random initialization using specialized input control tokens and output diff sequences (and associated output control tokens) that separated non-preferred (pre-transduction) and preferred (post-transduction) generated sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmaltz et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Importantly, the bias on the diff control sequences could be modulated to control precision and recall over the absence and presence of the transduction operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmaltz et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. In-effect, the sequence transduction model could be effectively used as a classifier without additional classification layers, while also having the expressivity to generate token sequences, unlike standard discrete classifiers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Input and output control tokens are now prominent features of LLM vocabularies to structure prompts, instructions, and reasoning sequences. However, while the bias of individual tokens can be modified with an additive offset, current LLMs lack a mechanism to explicitly bifurcate the output distribution into non-preferred and preferred regions in the manner of the earlier models. This capability can be (re)-added to LLMs without direct training on diff transduction sequences, as follows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Negative+Positive Vocabulary Normalization.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a pre-trained LLM model, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our reference model generates acceptable sequences over part of the data distribution, but it also produces non-preferred (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">negative</span>) generations; hence, our desire for further training. However, we only want to alter the behavior of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the space that produces <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">negative</span> generations, otherwise we may unexpectedly cause the previously acceptable space of generations to also become <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">negative</span>. In effect, we have two regions—a bifurcation—of the output distribution: The space of existing acceptable generations and the space of <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">negative</span> generations. We seek to replace the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">negative</span> region with a new <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">positive</span> region of acceptable generations without (or at least minimally) impacting the existing acceptable region.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> create two clones, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each model has a final linear layer that maps to the output vocabulary, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, via a weight matrix<span id="footnote13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">13</span>
                  
                  
                  
                We ignore the bias terms here to simplify the presentation. In practice, it is not uncommon for <math id="footnote13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{ref}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>ref</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{ref}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{neg}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>neg</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{neg}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{pos}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}}={\bm{W}}^{T}_{\rm{pos}}{\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. During fine-tuning for the next-token loss, we then calculate the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation (in-place of a standard <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math>) as the concatenation of the un-normalized output of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math>, keeping the weights of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, fixed and updating the weights of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> documents that participate in fine-tuning, we simply take the original token indexes and add an offset, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}+|{\mathcal{V}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}+|{\mathcal{V}}|</annotation></semantics></math>, for the output tokens when calculating the loss over the joint, concatenated distribution. (Input tokens retain their original indexes.) At test time, the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> output <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{index}~{\rm{mod}}~|{\mathcal{V}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>index</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{index}~{\rm{mod}}~|{\mathcal{V}}|</annotation></semantics></math> maps back to the original token symbol in the vocabulary. In this way, an additional set of token symbols is never explicitly instantiated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the most direct sense, this then requires a copy of the full weights to be present at test time. However, in practice, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be a copy of all the weights; <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by adaptor layers, or similar mechanisms (e.g., only updating a subset of the model’s weights).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regularization.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further prevent drift from the original reference distribution, we also add an <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization term in the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{(2+q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{(2+q)}</annotation></semantics></math> space of the normalized joint, concatenated distribution when calculating the next-token loss:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\regularizationTerm=||{\bm{i}}\odot\log_{(2+q)}\left(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})\right)-{\bm{i}}\odot\log_{(2+q)}\left(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})\right)||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\regularizationTerm=||{\bm{i}}\odot\log_{(2+q)}\left(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})\right)-{\bm{i}}\odot\log_{(2+q)}\left(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})\right)||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the Hadamard (element-wise) product (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math>) is with a mask vector <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒊</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}</annotation></semantics></math> that lessens the regularization on the peak of the distribution by not considering the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> indexes of the reference, negative, and positive distributions, as well as that of the ground-truth next-token label (here, represented as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), in the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> constraint:<span id="footnote14" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">14</span>
                  
                  
                  
                This also accounts for the setting, not considered in our experiments, where <math id="footnote14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not identical to <math id="footnote14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as via multiple iterations of fine-tuning where <math id="footnote14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained away and is replaced with <math id="footnote14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a subsequent fine-tuning round.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})}+|{\mathcal{V}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{ref}})}+|{\mathcal{V}}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})}+|{\mathcal{V}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{({\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})}+|{\mathcal{V}}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒊</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{i}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We seek for our regularization term to be scaled relative to the loss, so we perform a simple re-scaling:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\regularizationTerm^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\regularizationTerm^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\max(\regularizationTerm,1)^{\min(\max(s,0),1)}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\max(\regularizationTerm,1)^{\min(\max(s,0),1)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\log_{e}{{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}},\theta_{\rm{pos}};\trainSplit)}}{\log_{e}{\regularizationTerm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\log_{e}{{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}},\theta_{\rm{pos}};\trainSplit)}}{\log_{e}{\regularizationTerm}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">After rescaling, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\regularizationTerm^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\regularizationTerm^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an additive term in the next-token training loss, described below. Next, we describe the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activations, and the structure of the network, more generally.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3 </span>SDM network</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network makes use of two separate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activations. The first (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>) is over the binary verification task, trained at the document level. This is built as described in § <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to SDM Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, but specifically with an exemplar adaptor <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\left({\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{neg}}),{\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}),{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{4D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>neg</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\left({\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{neg}}),{\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}),{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{4D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, trained over the concatenation of the mean of the final hidden states across tokens of both <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{\rm{neg}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{\rm{neg}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the hidden state (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{neg}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>neg</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{neg}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>) that predicts the end of sequence delimiter<span id="footnote15" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">15</span>
                
                
                
              This is the symbol that indicates the end of the sequence at the unit of analysis of the verification labels (e.g., at the sentence or document level).</span></span></span>, for which we use the superscript -1, all of which remain fixed when training the adaptor.<span id="footnote16" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">16</span>
                
                
                
              In our small-scale experiments, we only train the final hidden layer of the LLM (i.e., <math id="footnote16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> stays fixed, and we only update <math id="footnote16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>), so we exclude the weights of <math id="footnote16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to the exemplar adaptor, since they are identical to those of <math id="footnote16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></span></span> This has an associated <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower</annotation></semantics></math>, over the binary verification task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation is for normalizing the linear layer over the output vocabulary for next-token training, as described in § <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2 Negative+Positive Vocabulary Normalization and Regularization ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a>. In this case, the output <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the concatenation of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math>, but the values of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> are from the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, for this second <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation, there is no exemplar adaptor inserted between the final hidden state of the LLM and the linear-layer over the vocabulary. This enables easily adapting this mechanism to existing architectures and pre-trained weights.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SDM Network Next-token Loss.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Holding the weights of the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, the next token loss to update the weights of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is then:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}},\theta_{\rm{pos}};\trainSplit,\beta,{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}})=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z_{{\rm{neg,pos}}_{t_{n}}}}}{\sum^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}_{v=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z_{{\rm{neg,pos}}_{v}}}}}\right)+\beta\regularizationTerm^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>neg</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>pos</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>neg</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>pos</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}},\theta_{\rm{pos}};\trainSplit,\beta,{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}})=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z_{{\rm{neg,pos}}_{t_{n}}}}}{\sum^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\cdot 2}_{v=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z_{{\rm{neg,pos}}_{v}}}}}\right)+\beta\regularizationTerm^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index of the correct next token, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> linearly increases every mini-batch in an epoch from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 0, in our experiments) to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\rm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\rm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 0.1, in our experiments).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Train-time Generation vs. Train-time Force-decoding.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The loss in Eq. <a href="#S4.E32" title="In SDM Network Next-token Loss. ‣ 4.3.3 SDM network ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a> requires <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, which are predicated on labels at the document level, for each token prior to the model seeing the end of the document. In practice, for each <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},y=1)\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},y=1)\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, prior to calculating the loss, we decode a completion for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> starting at the completion marker <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., starting at the instruction prompt, or given prefix, as noted in § <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS1" title="4.3.1 Universal Verification Encoding ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.1</span></a>) with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we derive <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> over this generated output. We otherwise discard the generated completion and calculate the loss using these updated values of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> over the correct next token. (In the present work, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> are the same for each token in a single document.) Note that the stored support set of the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> (which determines <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) is constructed by force-decoding over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},y=\{0,1\})\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},y=\{0,1\})\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the loss has the desired semantics of rewarding the model to resemble the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> data at the token-level (as in standard next-token fine-tuning), while penalizing generations that are challenging to verify.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SDM Network Training: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> + Next-token Loop.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The next-token loss and the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> interact via <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the stopping criteria. However, the weight updates of each occur separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We seek the weights that maximize the admitted points over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and (if available), further restricting this set to those with correct <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math> predictions (parsed from the generated text) for the underlying tasks encoded in the data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The combined training loop is conceptually straightforward (Alg. <a href="#alg4" title='Algorithm 4 ‣ SDM Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 SDM network ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). First, we construct the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator for binary verification (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>) via Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.2 From SDM Activation Functions to SDM Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by force-decoding over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. (The convention is to shuffle <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> in the first training of the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>, itself a process over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, and then use that final data split for all subsequent processes.) Next, we train one epoch of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The next-token loss (Eq. <a href="#S4.E32" title="In SDM Network Next-token Loss. ‣ 4.3.3 SDM network ‣ 4.3 From SDM Calibration to SDM Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) uses <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> over completions generated via greedy decoding (with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>) using <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math> starting at the completion marker.<span id="footnote17" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">17</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">17</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">17</span>
                  
                  
                  
                In our experiments, we cache <math id="footnote17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> before each epoch, but in principle, they can be calculated dynamically during an epoch as the weights change. Caching simplifies the implementation at the expense of potentially biasing the estimates as an epoch proceeds, which is the motivation for increasing <math id="footnote17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> through the course of an epoch.</span></span></span> Once the epoch concludes, we retrain the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> and update <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We then generate completions using <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and calculate the number of points for which <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EstimatorPLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EstimatorPLower</annotation></semantics></math> provides an index-conditional estimate for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, further restricted (if applicable) to the underlying task labels, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math>, and the predictions parsed for those tasks from the generated output. Next, we continue to the next epoch of updating <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This process continues until the max number of epochs has been reached.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span> <math id="alg4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Network Training</figcaption>
